// Tailscale access policy for the a11y-witness fleet.
//
// PASTE THIS into the admin console under Access Controls, replacing what is there. It lives in the repo
// because it is infrastructure configuration, and configuration nobody can review or diff is configuration
// that drifts — the same argument that put the fleet in `inventory.yml` and the lab jobs in a catalogue.
//
// ## Why this exists
//
// The four capture workers serve HTTP on :8765 with **no authentication of any kind**. That is deliberate
// and documented (`packages/worker-fleet/ansible/README.md`): a mutating route on an unauthenticated
// worker would be remote code execution on every box, which is why the fleet is managed over SSH instead.
//
// Safe while "on the LAN" was the trust boundary. Since 2026-08-23 the boundary is "on the tailnet", and
// Tailscale's default policy is a single allow-all grant — so without this, a phone added to the tailnet
// can drive a screen reader on four machines.
//
// ## `grants`, not `acls`
//
// Written in the grants syntax this tailnet was provisioned with. Tailscale REFUSES a policy containing
// both `acls` and `grants`, so an older-style file cannot simply be added alongside the default.
// ## The order matters, and only one sequence works
//
//   1. Save this policy. `tagOwners` must exist before any node can claim the tag.
//   2. `npm run fleet:tailscale -- -e tag=true` — re-runs tailscale up with
//      `--advertise-tags=tag:a11y-fleet`. This RE-AUTHENTICATES: applying a tag transfers the node from
//      your account to the tag, so a fresh login is unavoidable.
//   3. Routes then auto-approve, for this machine and any added later. No console visit.
//
// Doing 2 before 1 fails: a node cannot advertise a tag the tailnet has not declared.
//
// ## Nothing may follow the closing brace
//
// HuJSON allows comments INSIDE the object and nothing after it. A trailing note here produced
// `parsing comment: unexpected EOF` in the admin console — a syntax error that reads like a truncated
// file. Keep prose above the `{` or inside it.

{
  // Lets the subnet router hold a tag. A tag matters for more than tidiness: an untagged node is owned by
  // a user and inherits that user's KEY EXPIRY — default 180 days — after which it silently drops off the
  // tailnet. For a subnet router that everything else is reached through, that is an outage with a
  // six-month fuse. A tagged node does not expire.
  "tagOwners": {
    "tag:a11y-fleet": ["autogroup:admin"],
  },

  // Approve advertised routes automatically, inside this prefix and no wider.
  //
  // Without it, every machine added to `inventory.yml` needs a visit to the admin console before it is
  // reachable — a manual step in a workflow that repeats. Scoped to REDACTED-INTERNAL-ADDRESS/24 so the router can only
  // ever auto-approve routes on the home LAN, never become a path somewhere else.
  //
  // REQUIRES THE NODE TO ACTUALLY CARRY THE TAG. It is named here, and until
  // `tailscale up --advertise-tags=tag:a11y-fleet` has been run this rule matches nothing and approves
  // nothing — which looks exactly like being configured. See the sequence at the bottom of this file.
  "autoApprovers": {
    "routes": {
      "REDACTED-INTERNAL-ADDRESS/24": ["tag:a11y-fleet"],
    },
  },

  // Default DENY: anything not granted below is refused. The starter policy's `{"src":["*"],"dst":["*"]}`
  // is gone, and that is the point — leaving it means the tailnet's security is "every device I ever add
  // is trustworthy forever", a promise nobody can keep.
  "grants": [
    // Your devices reach the capture workers, on the worker port only.
    //
    // Port-scoped rather than everything: these are Windows boxes with plenty else listening, and :8765 is
    // the only port the tooling uses.
    {
      "src": ["autogroup:member"],
      "dst": ["REDACTED-INTERNAL-ADDRESS", "REDACTED-INTERNAL-ADDRESS", "REDACTED-INTERNAL-ADDRESS", "REDACTED-INTERNAL-ADDRESS"],
      "ip": ["tcp:8765"],
    },

    // SSH to the lab, the control container, and the subnet router itself.
    //
    // This is the rule that makes working away from home possible at all: `lab:job`, `lab:fetch` and
    // `lab:stop` are Ansible over SSH. The control container holds the fleet key (ADR 0012), so this stays
    // narrow — SSH, nothing else.
    //
    // The router is named THREE ways on purpose. `tag:a11y-fleet` is what it will be; `autogroup:self`
    // covers it while it is still owned by your account, before the tag is applied. Without the second,
    // saving this policy would cut off SSH to the router until the tag existed — and the tag can only be
    // applied over that same connection.
    {
      "src": ["autogroup:member"],
      "dst": ["REDACTED-INTERNAL-ADDRESS", "REDACTED-INTERNAL-ADDRESS", "tag:a11y-fleet", "autogroup:self"],
      "ip": ["tcp:22"],
    },

    // The AI server, on whatever it serves.
    //
    // Deliberately broad and flagged as such: it is not part of this project, its ports are not this
    // file's business, and inventing a port list would be a rule that looks precise and is guesswork.
    // Narrow it when its actual ports are known.
    {
      "src": ["autogroup:member"],
      "dst": ["REDACTED-INTERNAL-ADDRESS"],
      "ip": ["*"],
    },
  ],

  // KEPT FROM THE DEFAULT POLICY, unchanged.
  //
  // This governs Tailscale SSH — where Tailscale itself brokers the connection — which this project does
  // not use; the fleet runs ordinary sshd with the `a11y-pve` key. So it is inert for us, and dropping
  // someone else's working configuration because it is unfamiliar is not a tidy-up.
  "ssh": [
    {
      "action": "check",
      "src":    ["autogroup:member"],
      "dst":    ["autogroup:self"],
      "users":  ["autogroup:nonroot", "root"],
    },
  ],
}
